// Copyright (c) 2026 Jurjen Stellingwerff
// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// `t.0 += [x]` on a vector held in a TUPLE ELEMENT appends once.
//
// A vector literal on the right of `+=` is parsed with the LEFT-HAND PLACE as its accumulator.
// For a bare VARIABLE or a struct FIELD, `build_vector_list` builds the elements straight into
// that place and hands back a `Value::Insert` — and the `lhs += other_vec` concat below it is
// gated on `!matches!(code, Value::Insert(_))`, so it correctly does not fire.  That is why
// both of those place-kinds were always right.
//
// A TUPLE ELEMENT is neither, so the literal opens a BLOCK that ADOPTS the place's store
// (`_vec_N = t.0`) and builds into it.  A block is not an `Insert`, the gate misses it, and the
// concat then appends the destination to ITSELF.
//
// ⚠ The signature is the correct answer concatenated with itself, and it is what tells this
// apart from a wrong element or a miscount:
//
//     []      += [7]      ->  7 7                 (not [7])
//     [1, 2]  += [7]      ->  1 2 7 1 2 7         (not [1,2,7])
//     []      += [7, 8]   ->  7 8 7 8             (not [7,8])
//
// so a cell scoring only `len` on the first row sees 2 and could read it as one spurious
// element; the second row is what says the whole RESULT is duplicated.  All three are scored,
// with their ELEMENTS, for that reason.
//
// `(E-Asgn-Compound)` is the rule — the place is addressed exactly once — and here the place
// was the accumulator AND the append destination.  Same rule loft#1205 was about, at a tuple
// place rather than through a discharge.
//
// ⚠⚠ A tuple place has TWO IR spellings — a bare `TupleGet` at depth 1, and a
// `Block[Set(w, …), TupleGet(w, idx)]` chain deeper — and a gate written against the first
// alone fixes every row above while `n.0.0 += [7]` goes on answering `[7, 7]`.  Measured, in
// that order: QUALITY.md's `spellings` audit row moved when the narrow gate landed, and the
// cell built to answer WHY the row moved is the one that found the half-fix.  The shipped gate
// asks `extract_nested_tuple_lhs`, which is the one home for the question.
//
// ⚠⚠ The two CONTROLS are what make the cure narrow.  A vector LOCAL `v += [7]` and a plain
// `t.0 = [9]` into the same slot were both always correct; the fix must not touch either, and
// it does not — it only declines to emit a concat whose right operand has already been built
// into its left.
//
// NOT scored here, and open: the same statement on a `text` member (`t.0 += "cd"`) and on an
// `integer` member (`t.0 += 3`).  Those fail through a different mechanism — a compound assign
// at a tuple place has no write-back, so the text form appends into a minted work variable that
// is never stored and the scalar form is turned away by arithmetic dispatch.  They need the
// tuple slot's read-modify-write, which is not this change; tracked on loft#1228.
//
// @falsified-at: baa66fcc — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native
//   exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0.  One failure rather than several because the
//   run stops at the first: on the parent the first scored row answers len 2 with both elements
//   7, on both backends, and the two later rows sit behind it.  The two control cells pass on
//   the parent too, so a build that merely stopped emitting the concat would not satisfy them.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees. Falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on
//   both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

fn main() {
  // ---- the defect: append into a vector held in a tuple element ----
  a1228: (vector<integer>, integer) = ([], 5);
  a1228.0 += [7];
  assert(len(a1228.0) == 1, "one append adds one element");
  assert(a1228.0[0] == 7, "and it is the element appended");

  // ---- a NON-empty destination: the whole result was duplicated, not just the tail ----
  b1228: (vector<integer>, integer) = ([1, 2], 5);
  b1228.0 += [7];
  assert(len(b1228.0) == 3, "appending to a non-empty element keeps its length honest");
  assert(b1228.0[0] == 1, "the existing elements stay in place");
  assert(b1228.0[1] == 2, "both of them");
  assert(b1228.0[2] == 7, "with the appended one after");

  // ---- a MULTI-element literal, so the doubling cannot be read as one stray element ----
  c1228: (vector<integer>, integer) = ([], 5);
  c1228.0 += [7, 8];
  assert(len(c1228.0) == 2, "a two-element literal appends two");
  assert(c1228.0[0] == 7, "in order");
  assert(c1228.0[1] == 8, "both of them");

  // ---- BOTH IR SPELLINGS of a tuple place, and the second is not optional ----
  //
  // A tuple projection is a bare `TupleGet` at depth 1 and a `Block[Set(w, …), TupleGet(w, i)]`
  // chain deeper.  A gate written against `TupleGet` alone fixes the rows above and leaves this
  // one doubling — measured, exactly that — which is why the shipped gate asks
  // `extract_nested_tuple_lhs`, the one home for what a tuple place looks like.
  n1228: ((vector<integer>, integer), integer) = (([], 5), 9);
  n1228.0.0 += [7];
  assert(len(n1228.0.0) == 1, "a NESTED tuple place appends once too");
  assert(n1228.0.0[0] == 7, "with the element appended");

  o1228: ((vector<integer>, integer), integer) = (([1, 2], 5), 9);
  o1228.0.0 += [7];
  assert(len(o1228.0.0) == 3, "and a non-empty nested element keeps its length honest");
  assert(o1228.0.0[2] == 7, "with the appended element last");

  // ---- the OTHER tuple member is untouched by any of it ----
  assert(a1228.1 == 5, "the sibling member is unchanged");
  assert(b1228.1 == 5, "in every cell");

  // ---- CONTROL: the same append one place-kind over ----
  v1228: vector<integer> = [];
  v1228 += [7];
  assert(len(v1228) == 1, "a vector LOCAL was never affected");

  // ---- CONTROL: plain `=` into the same tuple slot ----
  d1228: (vector<integer>, integer) = ([1, 2], 5);
  d1228.0 = [9];
  assert(len(d1228.0) == 1, "`=` into a tuple element REPLACES, and was always correct");
  assert(d1228.0[0] == 9, "with the assigned value");
}
